IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `je54-nouns`
IMPORT `je54-vintage`

-- je54-art12-15-penalties-forms: Articles 12, 13, 14 and 15 of the Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, for a given TEXT of the Law.
-- The four Articles are word for word the same in all seven deposited texts.  Selecting date: the day the fact occurred (the day of the act or omission, of the penalty, of the form used, of the measurement);
-- the caller reads the text for that day.  A question about a day before 1 January 2019 refuses (no text is deposited).
-- Words differ between the Articles: 12, 13(1), 13(3) and 15 say "unless the contrary intention appears"; 13(2) and 14 do not.  Only the functions of the first kind take a contrary intention as an input.

§ `Article 12: Offences under 2 or more enactments, etc.`

-- src:66 | Where an act or omission constitutes an offence under 2 or more enactments, or both under an enactment and under the customary law of Jersey, whether any such enactment was passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the offender shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be liable to be prosecuted and punished under either or any of those enactments or under the customary law, but shall not be liable to be punished twice for the same offence.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 12 (src:66)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 12: a contrary intention appears, and the Article does not decide` MEANS
    REFUSE "a contrary intention appears: Article 12 does not say under what the offender is liable to be prosecuted and punished"

-- src:66 | Where an act or omission constitutes an offence under 2 or more enactments, or both under an enactment and under the customary law of Jersey, whether any such enactment was passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the offender shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be liable to be prosecuted and punished under either or any of those enactments or under the customary law, but shall not be liable to be punished twice for the same offence.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 12 (src:66)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 12: two offences under the same enactment are not dealt with` MEANS
    REFUSE "Article 12 says nothing about an act or omission that constitutes two offences under the same enactment"

-- src:66 | Where an act or omission constitutes an offence under 2 or more enactments, or both under an enactment and under the customary law of Jersey, whether any such enactment was passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the offender shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be liable to be prosecuted and punished under either or any of those enactments or under the customary law, but shall not be liable to be punished twice for the same offence.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 12 (src:66)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 12: there is no second source of the offence` MEANS
    REFUSE "Article 12 deals only where an act or omission constitutes an offence under 2 or more enactments, or both under an enactment and under the customary law of Jersey"

-- src:66 | Where an act or omission constitutes an offence under 2 or more enactments, or both under an enactment and under the customary law of Jersey, whether any such enactment was passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the offender shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be liable to be prosecuted and punished under either or any of those enactments or under the customary law, but shall not be liable to be punished twice for the same offence.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 12 (src:66)
GIVETH A STRING
`the customary law of Jersey` MEANS "the customary law of Jersey"

-- Article 12 applies where the act or omission is an offence under 2 or more enactments, or both under an enactment and under the customary law of Jersey.  "2 or more enactments" is read as 2 or more distinct enactments:
-- one enactment named twice is the case the Article does not deal with.
-- src:66 | Where an act or omission constitutes an offence under 2 or more enactments, or both under an enactment and under the customary law of Jersey, whether any such enactment was passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the offender shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be liable to be prosecuted and punished under either or any of those enactments or under the customary law, but shall not be liable to be punished twice for the same offence.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 12 (src:66)
GIVEN o IS AN `Offences arising from one act or omission`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 12: the offences have more than one source` o MEANS
        count (nub (o's `the enactments that create the offences`)) GREATER THAN 1
     OR (count (nub (o's `the enactments that create the offences`)) GREATER THAN 0 AND o's `it is also an offence under the customary law of Jersey`)

-- src:66 | Where an act or omission constitutes an offence under 2 or more enactments, or both under an enactment and under the customary law of Jersey, whether any such enactment was passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the offender shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be liable to be prosecuted and punished under either or any of those enactments or under the customary law, but shall not be liable to be punished twice for the same offence.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 12 (src:66)
GIVEN v IS A `Text of the Law`
      o IS AN `Offences arising from one act or omission`
      `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 12: the Article applies` v o `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the text of the Law is one of the seven deposited texts` v THEN `the Law has no deposited text for a day before the first consolidated version`
           IF `a contrary intention appears` THEN `Article 12: a contrary intention appears, and the Article does not decide`
           IF `Article 12: the offences have more than one source` o THEN TRUE
           IF count (o's `the enactments that create the offences`) GREATER THAN 1 THEN `Article 12: two offences under the same enactment are not dealt with`
           OTHERWISE `Article 12: there is no second source of the offence`

-- "the offender shall ... be liable to be prosecuted and punished under either or any of those enactments or under the customary law": each enactment named, and the customary law if the act or omission is an offence under it.
-- src:66 | Where an act or omission constitutes an offence under 2 or more enactments, or both under an enactment and under the customary law of Jersey, whether any such enactment was passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the offender shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be liable to be prosecuted and punished under either or any of those enactments or under the customary law, but shall not be liable to be punished twice for the same offence.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 12 (src:66)
GIVEN v IS A `Text of the Law`
      o IS AN `Offences arising from one act or omission`
      route IS A STRING
      `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 12: the offender is liable to be prosecuted and punished under` v o route `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
        `Article 12: the Article applies` v o `a contrary intention appears`
    AND (   elem route (o's `the enactments that create the offences`)
         OR (route EQUALS `the customary law of Jersey` AND o's `it is also an offence under the customary law of Jersey`))

-- "but shall not be liable to be punished twice for the same offence".  Whether two punishments are for "the same offence" is the caller's judgement; the Law does not say.
-- src:66 | Where an act or omission constitutes an offence under 2 or more enactments, or both under an enactment and under the customary law of Jersey, whether any such enactment was passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the offender shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be liable to be prosecuted and punished under either or any of those enactments or under the customary law, but shall not be liable to be punished twice for the same offence.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 12 (src:66)
GIVEN v IS A `Text of the Law`
      o IS AN `Offences arising from one act or omission`
      `the offender has already been punished for the same offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 12: the offender is liable to be punished` v o `the offender has already been punished for the same offence` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
        `Article 12: the Article applies` v o `a contrary intention appears`
    AND NOT `the offender has already been punished for the same offence`

§ `Article 13: Construction of penalties for offences`

-- src:68 | (1) Where, in an enactment, a penalty is specified in respect of an offence, unless the contrary intention appears, the offence shall be punishable by a penalty not exceeding the penalty specified.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 13(1) (src:68)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 13(1): no penalty is specified` MEANS
    REFUSE "Article 13(1) deals only where, in an enactment, a penalty is specified in respect of an offence"

-- src:68 | (1) Where, in an enactment, a penalty is specified in respect of an offence, unless the contrary intention appears, the offence shall be punishable by a penalty not exceeding the penalty specified.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 13(1) (src:68)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 13(1): a contrary intention appears, and the Article does not decide` MEANS
    REFUSE "a contrary intention appears: Article 13(1) does not say what penalty the offence is then punishable by"

-- 13(1): the offence is punishable by a penalty not exceeding the penalty specified.  The two penalties are in one unit (years, days, pounds); the Law does not compare penalties of different kinds.
-- src:68 | (1) Where, in an enactment, a penalty is specified in respect of an offence, unless the contrary intention appears, the offence shall be punishable by a penalty not exceeding the penalty specified.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 13(1) (src:68)
GIVEN v IS A `Text of the Law`
      `a penalty is specified in respect of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the penalty specified` IS A NUMBER
      `the penalty proposed` IS A NUMBER
      `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 13(1): the offence is punishable by the penalty proposed` v `a penalty is specified in respect of the offence` `the penalty specified` `the penalty proposed` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the text of the Law is one of the seven deposited texts` v THEN `the Law has no deposited text for a day before the first consolidated version`
           IF NOT `a penalty is specified in respect of the offence` THEN `Article 13(1): no penalty is specified`
           IF `a contrary intention appears` THEN `Article 13(1): a contrary intention appears, and the Article does not decide`
           OTHERWISE NOT (`the penalty proposed` GREATER THAN `the penalty specified`)

-- src:69 | (2) Where a penalty for an offence is a fine and the amount of the fine or a level on the standard scale is not specified, the fine shall be construed as a fine of an unlimited amount.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 13(2) (src:69)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 13(2): the penalty is not a fine` MEANS
    REFUSE "Article 13(2) deals only where a penalty for an offence is a fine"

-- 13(2): where a penalty for an offence is a fine and the amount of the fine or a level on the standard scale is not specified, the fine is construed as a fine of an unlimited amount.
-- Read as: neither an amount nor a level is specified (otherwise every fine with an amount but no level would be unlimited).  Article 13(2) has no "unless the contrary intention appears", so there is no such input.
-- FALSE means that Article 13(2) does not make the fine unlimited because an amount or a level is specified; Article 13(1) then governs.
-- src:69 | (2) Where a penalty for an offence is a fine and the amount of the fine or a level on the standard scale is not specified, the fine shall be construed as a fine of an unlimited amount.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 13(2) (src:69)
GIVEN v IS A `Text of the Law`
      `the penalty is a fine` IS A BOOLEAN
      how IS A `How a fine is specified`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 13(2): the fine is of an unlimited amount` v `the penalty is a fine` how MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the text of the Law is one of the seven deposited texts` v THEN `the Law has no deposited text for a day before the first consolidated version`
           IF NOT `the penalty is a fine` THEN `Article 13(2): the penalty is not a fine`
           OTHERWISE
               CONSIDER how
               WHEN `neither an amount nor a level on the standard scale is specified` THEN TRUE
               OTHERWISE FALSE

-- src:70 | (3) Where, in an enactment, more than one penalty is specified for an offence, the use of the word “and” shall, unless the contrary intention appears, mean that the penalties may be imposed alternatively or cumulatively.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 13(3) (src:70)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 13(3): not several penalties joined by the word and` MEANS
    REFUSE "Article 13(3) deals only where, in an enactment, more than one penalty is specified for an offence and the word and is used; it says nothing of the word or or of any other way of joining penalties"

-- src:70 | (3) Where, in an enactment, more than one penalty is specified for an offence, the use of the word “and” shall, unless the contrary intention appears, mean that the penalties may be imposed alternatively or cumulatively.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 13(3) (src:70)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 13(3): a contrary intention appears, and the Article does not decide` MEANS
    REFUSE "a contrary intention appears: Article 13(3) does not say how the penalties may then be imposed"

-- 13(3): where more than one penalty is specified for an offence, the use of the word "and" means, unless the contrary intention appears, that the penalties may be imposed alternatively or cumulatively.
-- src:70 | (3) Where, in an enactment, more than one penalty is specified for an offence, the use of the word “and” shall, unless the contrary intention appears, mean that the penalties may be imposed alternatively or cumulatively.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 13(3) (src:70)
GIVEN v IS A `Text of the Law`
      `more than one penalty is specified and the word and is used` IS A BOOLEAN
      mode IS A `Mode of imposing several penalties`
      `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 13(3): the penalties may be imposed` v `more than one penalty is specified and the word and is used` mode `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the text of the Law is one of the seven deposited texts` v THEN `the Law has no deposited text for a day before the first consolidated version`
           IF NOT `more than one penalty is specified and the word and is used` THEN `Article 13(3): not several penalties joined by the word and`
           IF `a contrary intention appears` THEN `Article 13(3): a contrary intention appears, and the Article does not decide`
           OTHERWISE
               CONSIDER mode
               WHEN `alternatively` THEN TRUE
               WHEN `cumulatively` THEN TRUE

-- src:71 | (4) This Article shall apply to all enactments, whenever passed or made.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 13(4) (src:71)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 13: the Article does not say whether it applies to something that is not an enactment` MEANS
    REFUSE "Article 13 applies to enactments (Article 13(4)); it does not say whether it applies to something that is not an enactment"

-- 13(4): the Article applies to all enactments, whenever passed or made.  The day on which the enactment was passed or made is accepted and ignored.
-- src:71 | (4) This Article shall apply to all enactments, whenever passed or made.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 13(4) (src:71)
GIVEN v IS A `Text of the Law`
      k IS A `Kind of instrument`
      `the day on which the enactment was passed or made` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 13(4): the Article applies to an enactment passed or made on any day` v k `the day on which the enactment was passed or made` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the text of the Law is one of the seven deposited texts` v THEN `the Law has no deposited text for a day before the first consolidated version`
           IF k EQUALS `something that is none of these` THEN `Article 13: the Article does not say whether it applies to something that is not an enactment`
           OTHERWISE TRUE

§ `Article 14: Deviation in forms`

-- src:73 | Where a form is prescribed or specified by an enactment, deviations from that form not materially affecting the substance nor likely to mislead shall not invalidate the form used.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 14 (src:73)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 14: no form is prescribed or specified by an enactment` MEANS
    REFUSE "Article 14 deals only where a form is prescribed or specified by an enactment"

-- src:73 | Where a form is prescribed or specified by an enactment, deviations from that form not materially affecting the substance nor likely to mislead shall not invalidate the form used.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 14 (src:73)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 14: the form used does not deviate` MEANS
    REFUSE "Article 14 deals only with a deviation from the form prescribed or specified"

-- Article 14: deviations from the form not materially affecting the substance nor likely to mislead do not invalidate the form used.  Both conditions are judgements, made by the caller.  If either fails, the Article
-- does not save the form used; it does not say that the form is invalid, which is for the enactment.  Article 14 has no "unless the contrary intention appears", so there is no such input.
-- src:73 | Where a form is prescribed or specified by an enactment, deviations from that form not materially affecting the substance nor likely to mislead shall not invalidate the form used.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 14 (src:73)
GIVEN v IS A `Text of the Law`
      `a form is prescribed or specified by an enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the form used deviates from it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the deviation materially affects the substance` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the deviation is likely to mislead` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Effect of a deviation from a form`
`Article 14: the effect of the deviation from the form` v `a form is prescribed or specified by an enactment` `the form used deviates from it` `the deviation materially affects the substance` `the deviation is likely to mislead` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the text of the Law is one of the seven deposited texts` v THEN `the Law has no deposited text for a day before the first consolidated version`
           IF NOT `a form is prescribed or specified by an enactment` THEN `Article 14: no form is prescribed or specified by an enactment`
           IF NOT `the form used deviates from it` THEN `Article 14: the form used does not deviate`
           IF `the deviation materially affects the substance` OR `the deviation is likely to mislead` THEN `Article 14 does not save the form used, and does not say that it is invalid`
           OTHERWISE `the deviation does not invalidate the form used`

§ `Article 15: Measurement of distance`

-- src:75 | In the measurement of any distance for the purposes of any enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, that distance shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be measured in a straight line on a horizontal plane.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 15 (src:75)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 15: a contrary intention appears, and the Article does not decide` MEANS
    REFUSE "a contrary intention appears: Article 15 does not say how the distance is then to be measured"

-- Article 15: in the measurement of any distance for the purposes of any enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of the Law, the distance is, unless the contrary intention appears, measured in a straight
-- line on a horizontal plane.  The Law gives a method and no formula, so no geometry is done here: the way the distance was measured is a fact, and the measured distance is an input.  TRUE means that the distance as
-- measured is the distance for the purposes of the enactment, on the method the Article gives; FALSE means that it was measured another way and the Article, if it applies, does not accept it.
-- src:75 | In the measurement of any distance for the purposes of any enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, that distance shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be measured in a straight line on a horizontal plane.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 15 (src:75)
GIVEN v IS A `Text of the Law`
      d IS A `Distance as measured`
      `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 15: the distance as measured is the distance for the purposes of the enactment` v d `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the text of the Law is one of the seven deposited texts` v THEN `the Law has no deposited text for a day before the first consolidated version`
           IF `a contrary intention appears` THEN `Article 15: a contrary intention appears, and the Article does not decide`
           OTHERWISE d's `the way it was measured` EQUALS `in a straight line on a horizontal plane`
